{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"bc15977c604868b5","name":"card_unit_whirlwind","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/WorldAtWar/units/card_unit_whirlwind","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"WHIRLWIND","text":"When the enemy targets or attacks this unit, deal 3 damage to their HQ.","flavorText":"The Westland Whirlwind was Britain’s first single-seat, twin-engine fighter and the first RAF aircraft armed with four 20 mm cannons. Introduced in 1940, it excelled at low-level attack missions thanks to its speed, firepower, and agility. Only 114 were built due to engine production limits, yet it served effectively with two RAF squadrons. The Whirlwind was first used by 263 Squadron for convoy patrols and coastal defense, before shifting to offensive operations across the English Channel. It hunted German aircraft, strafed trains and sank enemy ships. In 1941, 137 Squadron was formed to exploit the aircraft’s precision and power in attacking railways and other transport targets. Armed with two 500 lb bombs, the “Whirlibomber” version flew cross-channel sorties, often returning with multiple kills. Pilots praised the Whirlwind’s heavy nose-mounted cannon, superb low-altitude performance and toughness. It was fast enough to contend with enemy fighters and rugged enough to survive crash landings. However, the unreliable Rolls-Royce Peregrine engine ended its career. By late 1943, the Whirlwind was retired in favor of the Hawker Typhoon.","faction":"Britain","type":"fighter","rarity":"Unique","cost":6,"operationCost":2,"attack":6,"defense":6},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_whirlwind","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_whirlwind_title","SourceString":"WHIRLWIND","LocalizedString":"WHIRLWIND"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::fighter","attack":6,"defense":6,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":6,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_whirlwind_text","SourceString":"When the enemy targets or attacks this unit, deal 3 damage to their HQ.","LocalizedString":"When the enemy targets or attacks this unit, deal 3 damage to their HQ."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_whirlwind_history","SourceString":"The Westland Whirlwind was Britain’s first single-seat, twin-engine fighter and the first RAF aircraft armed with four 20 mm cannons. Introduced in 1940, it excelled at low-level attack missions thanks to its speed, firepower, and agility. Only 114 were built due to engine production limits, yet it served effectively with two RAF squadrons. The Whirlwind was first used by 263 Squadron for convoy patrols and coastal defense, before shifting to offensive operations across the English Channel. It hunted German aircraft, strafed trains and sank enemy ships. In 1941, 137 Squadron was formed to exploit the aircraft’s precision and power in attacking railways and other transport targets. Armed with two 500 lb bombs, the “Whirlibomber” version flew cross-channel sorties, often returning with multiple kills. Pilots praised the Whirlwind’s heavy nose-mounted cannon, superb low-altitude performance and toughness. It was fast enough to contend with enemy fighters and rugged enough to survive crash landings. However, the unreliable Rolls-Royce Peregrine engine ended its career. By late 1943, the Whirlwind was retired in favor of the Hawker Typhoon.","LocalizedString":"The Westland Whirlwind was Britain’s first single-seat, twin-engine fighter and the first RAF aircraft armed with four 20 mm cannons. Introduced in 1940, it excelled at low-level attack missions thanks to its speed, firepower, and agility. Only 114 were built due to engine production limits, yet it served effectively with two RAF squadrons. The Whirlwind was first used by 263 Squadron for convoy patrols and coastal defense, before shifting to offensive operations across the English Channel. It hunted German aircraft, strafed trains and sank enemy ships. In 1941, 137 Squadron was formed to exploit the aircraft’s precision and power in attacking railways and other transport targets. Armed with two 500 lb bombs, the “Whirlibomber” version flew cross-channel sorties, often returning with multiple kills. Pilots praised the Whirlwind’s heavy nose-mounted cannon, superb low-altitude performance and toughness. It was fast enough to contend with enemy fighters and rugged enough to survive crash landings. However, the unreliable Rolls-Royce Peregrine engine ended its career. By late 1943, the Whirlwind was retired in favor of the Hawker Typhoon."},"operationCost":2,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnBeforeOtherCardAttacks","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnBeforeOtherCardPlayedFromHand"],"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Britain/World_at_War/T_whirlwind.T_whirlwind","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Michael Turner","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/WHIRLWIND.WHIRLWIND","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":384.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_whirlwind_photo","SourceString":"A Westland Whirlwind in flight. Ca. 1940-42.","LocalizedString":"A Westland Whirlwind in flight. Ca. 1940-42."},"blowUpExplosion":{"ObjectName":"ParticleSystem'P_ExplosionAircraftL'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Explosions/Large/Aircraft/P_ExplosionAircraftL.201"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeHeavy_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/examples/GrenadePack/Blueprints/CameraShakeHeavy.0"},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackFighter.BP_AttackFighter_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_unit_whirlwind","owner":"card_unit_whirlwind_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final |